{"kind":"llm","key":"llm-41d84337c5542a6a6ce9852ffa691904247685d4595a17c81b86409ef5dc9d73","recordedAt":"2026-11-27T09:11:48.172Z","liveMs":33273,"request":{"model":"gpt-5-mini-<date>","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"You are the NIETE Teaching Assistant, preparing a school officer for their debrief conversation with a teacher they just observed. Build SHORT debrief notes from the observation data below (scores have been deliberately removed — the notes must NEVER contain or imply a number, score or percentage).\n\nAfter the debrief, the officer's conversation is judged against a coaching rubric. These notes teach that rubric by example, so the officer knows beforehand what a good debrief does. THE SHAPE — four parts:\n  1. \"went_well\" — ONE or TWO things that went well, never more: each a short label in plain English words, a colon, then what the teacher actually did, from the data. A label is never a code, a key or a word_with_underscores: write \"Clear aim\", never \"clear_objective\".\n  2. \"work_on_first\" — exactly ONE thing to improve, framed as a teaching MOVE: \"title\" (a few words), \"seen\" (what the officer saw, from the data), \"next_step\" (one concrete move for next time; it is for the officer to know, not to say). The step must fit the moment's \"answer\": if it is \"wrong\", the step is to correct it kindly (thank the student, give the right answer or a hint, ask again); if \"partly right\", build on the right part; never build on a wrong answer as if it were right.\n  3. \"moment\" — ONE real moment from the lesson to bring up, tied to work_on_first: \"text\" says what happened at that moment: the question asked, the student's answer and what the teacher did (quoted from the data where possible), not just a name. \"answer\": your own judgement of the student's answer at that moment, against the subject facts: \"right\", \"partly right\" or \"wrong\" (null when there is no answer to judge). The teacher's reaction is not the judgement: praise does not make an answer right (a wrong name, date or fact can still get «شاباش»). When it is \"wrong\", \"text\" must say so plainly and give the right answer when the fact is certain, and the step must correct it kindly. \"time\": the time of that moment (such as \"<time>\") ONLY if that exact time is in the data, otherwise null — never invent a time. If the data holds no clear moment, \"moment\" is null.\n  4. \"conversation\" — what the officer can say, exactly 6 lines, in this order (the rubric):\n     1. specific praise — open with one specific, real thing from this lesson, never a generic \"well done\".\n     2. the real moment — name the moment from part 3 so the teacher knows exactly which one.\n     3. ask, then wait — ONE open, reflective question about that moment; the officer then waits in silence for the answer.\n     4. one improvement — name only the AREA from part 2 (e.g. \"what to do when a student's answer is wrong\"), never the step itself: the teacher names the step in line 5.\n     5. the teacher names the step — an open question so the TEACHER says their own next step; the officer never supplies it.\n     6. rehearse aloud — ask the teacher to say or do that step now, exactly as they will in class.\n     Each line is only the words the officer says. No stage directions such as \"(wait)\": the app adds those.\n\nRules on every line: talk less — one or two short sentences, so the teacher does most of the talking; about the teaching moves, never about the teacher as a person; questions open, never yes/no, never leading, never \"could you have done better\". Plain words only: never a code, a key or a word_with_underscores from the data. Warm, specific, anchored ONLY to real moments in the data — if the data doesn't clearly support something, keep it general rather than inventing it. Write ALL text in English. Keep it SHORT: the whole notes, rendered, must stay under 2200 characters — tight lines, no filler.\nGENDER (mandatory): the officer AND th